Yes. For your pipeline, use Azure SQL as the source of truth:

Difference	Action
Exists in SQL, missing from Redis	Report it—it may indicate replication delay or a failed write
Exists in Redis, missing from SQL	Candidate for deletion from Redis
Exists in both	Keep it

Do not delete SQL rows just because Redis is missing them.

Add this utility inside RedisClientManager. It uses your existing Lettuce connection, scans only your table’s key prefix, and defaults to a dry run.

Run it between tests, with test writes stopped and RDI caught up. The comparison is not an atomic snapshot across both databases.

import io.lettuce.core.KeyScanCursor;
import io.lettuce.core.ScanArgs;
import io.lettuce.core.ScanCursor;
import io.lettuce.core.api.sync.RedisCommands;
import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;
public record CleanupReport(
    Set<String> sqlOnlyKeys,
    Set<String> redisOnlyKeys,
    long deletedKeys,
    long skippedKeys
) {}

Add the methods:

// Default: compare only. No deletion.
public CleanupReport reconcileTestKeys(
    DataSource sqlPool,
    String keyPrefix
) throws SQLException {
    return reconcileTestKeys(sqlPool, keyPrefix, false);
}
public CleanupReport reconcileTestKeys(
    DataSource sqlPool,
    String keyPrefix,
    boolean deleteRedisOnly
) throws SQLException {
    // Require a literal, non-empty table-specific prefix.
    if (keyPrefix == null || keyPrefix.isBlank()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "A table-specific Redis key prefix is required"
        );
    }
    for (String character : new String[]{"*", "?", "[", "]", "\\"}) {
        if (keyPrefix.contains(character)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Key prefix must not contain SCAN pattern characters"
            );
        }
    }
    RedisCommands<String, String> redis = connection.sync();
    Set<String> expectedKeys = new HashSet<>();
    Set<String> actualKeys = new HashSet<>();
    long skipped = 0;
    long deleted = 0;
    try (Connection sql = sqlPool.getConnection()) {
        sql.setAutoCommit(true);
        // Change the table name to your actual source table.
        try (PreparedStatement statement = sql.prepareStatement(
                "SELECT id FROM dbo.RDI_TEST")) {
            statement.setQueryTimeout(30);
            try (ResultSet rows = statement.executeQuery()) {
                while (rows.next()) {
                    expectedKeys.add(
                        keyPrefix + rows.getLong("id")
                    );
                }
            }
        }
        ScanArgs scanArgs = ScanArgs.Builder
            .matches(keyPrefix + "*")
            .limit(500);
        ScanCursor cursor = ScanCursor.INITIAL;
        do {
            KeyScanCursor<String> page =
                redis.scan(cursor, scanArgs);
            for (String key : page.getKeys()) {
                // Restrict cleanup to the exact prefix + numeric ID format.
                if (!key.startsWith(keyPrefix)) {
                    skipped++;
                    continue;
                }
                try {
                    long id = Long.parseLong(
                        key.substring(keyPrefix.length())
                    );
                    if (!key.equals(keyPrefix + id)) {
                        skipped++;
                        continue;
                    }
                    actualKeys.add(key);
                } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                    skipped++;
                }
            }
            cursor = page;
        } while (!cursor.isFinished());
        Set<String> sqlOnly = new HashSet<>(expectedKeys);
        sqlOnly.removeAll(actualKeys);
        Set<String> redisOnly = new HashSet<>(actualKeys);
        redisOnly.removeAll(expectedKeys);
        if (deleteRedisOnly) {
            // Recheck SQL immediately before each deletion.
            try (PreparedStatement lookup = sql.prepareStatement(
                    "SELECT 1 FROM dbo.RDI_TEST WHERE id = ?")) {
                lookup.setQueryTimeout(30);
                for (String key : redisOnly) {
                    long id = Long.parseLong(
                        key.substring(keyPrefix.length())
                    );
                    lookup.setLong(1, id);
                    boolean nowExistsInSql;
                    try (ResultSet row = lookup.executeQuery()) {
                        nowExistsInSql = row.next();
                    }
                    if (nowExistsInSql) {
                        skipped++;
                        continue;
                    }
                    deleted += redis.unlink(key);
                }
            }
        }
        return new CleanupReport(
            Set.copyOf(sqlOnly),
            Set.copyOf(redisOnly),
            deleted,
            skipped
        );
    }
}

SCAN incrementally visits keys; the set removes duplicate scan results. UNLINK removes a key and reclaims its memory asynchronously, including RedisJSON keys. 

First, review the differences:

var report = redisClient.reconcileTestKeys(
    pool,
    config.getRedisKeyPrefix()
);
System.out.println(
    "SQL only: " + report.sqlOnlyKeys().size()
);
System.out.println(
    "Redis only: " + report.redisOnlyKeys().size()
);
report.redisOnlyKeys().stream()
    .limit(20)
    .forEach(System.out::println);

After reviewing, enable deletion:

var report = redisClient.reconcileTestKeys(
    pool,
    config.getRedisKeyPrefix(),
    true
);
System.out.println("Deleted: " + report.deletedKeys());
System.out.println("Skipped: " + report.skippedKeys());

Use the exact table-specific prefix from buildRedisKey(). The SQL queries must cover the same rows as your RDI mapping—apply the same filters if RDI only replicates part of the table.

This is a simple in-memory utility suitable for your test dataset. For a clustered connection, confirm its SCAN covers the whole logical database. The fresh SQL check reduces stale deletions, but concurrent writes can still race with cleanup; stop test writers before running it.